A hidden, experimental `--member-invariants` option, requiring `--type-system-refresh`, begins member invariants (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/105). A datatype, class, trait, newtype or abstract type may declare `invariant e` members; they are parsed, printed and resolved as ghost predicates of the type, with the plan's reads restriction (an invariant depends on no mutable state); an invariant is never opaque. A codatatype may not declare an invariant, and a program, libraries included, that declares a member invariant may not declare a codatatype. For a datatype with invariants, `raw D` names the type of all its values, and `D` the subset of them that satisfy the invariants: constructor applications and updates have type `raw D`, and the members that an invariant applies to a raw value are inferred to be raw members, in which `this` has type `raw D`. The invariants of an inductive datatype with no parent traits, outside a refining module, are verified: converting a `raw D` to `D`, and applying a member that is not raw to a `raw D`, are checked against each invariant. For a generic datatype, `D<A>` is a subtype of `D<B>` only where `A` and `B` are the same type, whatever variance `D` declares, since an invariant may quantify over a type parameter; a conversion between them is checked, and a type that uses `D<X>` cannot declare `X` co- or contravariant (`raw D` keeps the declared variance). Such a datatype may declare one `witness e` (or `ghost witness e`) member, which makes `D` auto-initializable (or nonempty), and gives compiled code its default value; without one, `D` is possibly empty. The resolver checks that an invariant does not refer to the set of values of its own type (the plan's extension condition), conservatively, with the dependence of each type argument of a generic type that the invariant reaches. The invariants of a class with no parent traits, outside a refining module, are verified too: a constructor checks them at `new;`, before which the object is a `raw C`, and every non-null value of `C` or `C?` satisfies them; a class with invariants must declare a constructor and may not declare a witness; `raw C` is a subtype of nothing but `raw C?`, so a raw reference reaches `object`, a trait or a collection of those only where it is proved to satisfy the invariants; `o is C` tests the dynamic type alone, and a test of a raw reference is ghost. An invariant is revealed wherever its type is, and nowhere else. A program that declares any other invariant is not verified: the verifier reports those invariants as not yet supported, and compiled code ignores invariants. `raw` for a trait, newtype or abstract type, and witness members of traits, newtypes and abstract types, are parsed but not yet supported. A `.doo` library built with the option requires it of its clients; one built without it is unchanged. Without the option, parsing, diagnostics and the generated Boogie are unchanged.
